Based on the search results, the "BlueEfficiency" designation was used by Mercedes-Benz on previous generations (like the C218 CLS, roughly 2011-2017) to denote models with specific fuel-saving technologies (e.g., engine tweaks, aerodynamics, start/stop). This badge indicated a focus on reduced fuel consumption and emissions.

However, for the *most recent* generation of the CLS (C257, launched in 2018), the "BlueEfficiency" badge is no longer used. Efficiency technologies like mild-hybrid systems (EQ Boost) and optimized engines are now integrated into the standard model lineup without the specific "BlueEfficiency" branding. The badge was reportedly discontinued partly due to potential confusion with "BlueTec" diesel technology and because efficiency measures became more standard.

Therefore, evaluating the "most recent version" of a "CLS BlueEfficiency" means evaluating the closest equivalent in the current lineup, which would be the **base model CLS**. This model incorporates the latest efficiency technologies standard for the generation, fulfilling the *spirit* of the old BlueEfficiency designation.

